#!/usr/bin/env bash
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pull.fm alert sender. THE notification channel, and the only one.
#
#   pullfm-alert --key <dedupe-key> --title <title> [options] [--message <text>]
#   pullfm-alert --resolve --key <dedupe-key>     # the condition went away
#   pullfm-alert --ack <dedupe-key> [--ack-note <text>]   # a HUMAN saw it
#   pullfm-alert --list                           # what is pending, and how old
#
# Everything that wants to tell the operator something goes through this file,
# so there is exactly one place that knows what the channel is, one place that
# knows how to authenticate to it, and one place to change when it moves.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY --ack EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT `rm`
#
# The heartbeat publishes a count of UNACKNOWLEDGED conditions and the external
# dead man's switch raises "N unacknowledged alert(s)" from it. Until this verb
# existed there was no supported way to acknowledge one: the only way to clear a
# condition that was seen and understood but not yet fixed was to delete its
# dedupe stamp under /var/lib by hand.
#
# That is a training defect, not an inconvenience. A monitor that demands
# acknowledgement while offering only `rm` teaches its operator to delete files
# under /var/lib by reflex, and the reflex does not distinguish the probe you
# recognise from the real alert you have not read yet. Eventually a real one gets
# cleared unread, and nothing anywhere records that it was.
#
# So --ack RECORDS rather than unlinks: who acknowledged, when, from where, and
# an optional note. The record is the point. `--resolve` remains the verb for a
# condition that actually went away, and the two are deliberately different:
# resolve means fixed, ack means seen.
#
# THREE INVARIANTS, because this touches the dedupe marker:
#
#   1. THE DEDUPE STAMP IS NEVER TOUCHED BY AN ACK. /var/lib/pullfm/alerts/<key>
#      still holds exactly one Unix timestamp and nothing else, so the repeat
#      window keeps working and `exit 5` keeps meaning suppressed-not-failed.
#      An ack is a SIBLING file, <key>.ack. Acknowledging must not turn a
#      suppressed duplicate into a fresh page every minute.
#   2. AN ACK IS CLEARED WHEN THE CONDITION ACTUALLY FIRES AGAIN. Suppressed
#      repeats inside the window do not clear it; a real re-fire after the window
#      does. So an ack silences the nagging without silencing the condition, and
#      a problem that is still there comes back rather than staying acknowledged
#      forever.
#   3. ACKING SOMETHING THAT IS NOT PENDING FAILS LOUDLY (exit 6). A typo that
#      appeared to succeed would be the same defect in a new place: an operator
#      who believes they acknowledged something they did not.
#
# Age is tracked in a second sibling, <key>.first, written once when a key is
# first seen and never rewritten. It exists because a condition that re-fires
# every hour keeps a FRESH dedupe timestamp forever, so the dedupe stamp cannot
# answer "how long has this been broken". Without a first-seen time an
# unacknowledged alert sits at the same count indefinitely and never escalates,
# which is how a permanent alarm becomes wallpaper.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THE CHANNEL IS NOT IN THIS FILE
#
# The endpoint arrives at runtime from /etc/pullfm/alert.env, written by
# infra/observability/install-alert-env.sh out of 1Password, and never touches
# git. For most providers the URL IS the credential - a Discord or Slack webhook
# URL is a bearer token with a hostname in front of it, and on an anonymous ntfy
# the topic name is the whole access control - so it is handled as a secret
# whatever is configured.
#
# The TRANSPORT is no longer hardcoded either. PULLFM_ALERT_SINK_KIND selects the
# wire format, so moving Pull.fm's alerting to a different provider is one
# 1Password value and no code change. See DECISIONS.md SD-003.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE PRIMARY PATH IS NOT IN THIS FILE AT ALL, AND THAT IS THE POINT
#
# This file PUSHES. A push from the node cannot report the node being gone, and
# for most of this project's life that was the only path, which meant silence and
# health were indistinguishable. The primary path is now a PULL: pullfm-heartbeat
# writes a content-free beat, nginx serves it, and a scheduled GitHub Actions
# workflow outside every machine we own reads it and raises an issue when it
# stops. Nothing on this node can suppress that.
#
# So this file is now the LOW-LATENCY path, not the reliable one, and the
# division of labour is deliberate: it tells the operator quickly when it can,
# and the external watcher tells them eventually whatever happens here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FIVE SURFACES, IN ORDER OF HOW MUCH CONFIGURATION THEY NEED
#
#   1. The journal, at error priority, tagged `pullfm-alert`, always. Costs
#      nothing and exists on any node.
#   2. The spool, /var/log/pullfm/alerts.jsonl, always. One JSON object per
#      alert including whether delivery worked, so "nobody was told" is itself
#      a recorded fact rather than an absence of one.
#   3. The dedupe stamp in /var/lib/pullfm/alerts/, always - which is what
#      pullfm-heartbeat counts, so EVERY alert reaches the external watcher even
#      with no sink configured at all. This is why an unconfigured sink is no
#      longer the same thing as an unreachable operator.
#   4. The push sink, when alert.env configures one.
#   5. The caller's exit code, so a watchdog can tell a delivered alert from an
#      undelivered one and does not have to guess.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DEDUPLICATION IS NOT A NICETY HERE
#
# The watchdog runs every minute. A condition that stays true for a day is 1440
# notifications, and an operator who mutes the topic has exactly the same
# coverage as an operator who never armed it. Every alert therefore carries a
# --key, and a key that fired inside PULLFM_ALERT_REPEAT_S (default 3600) is
# suppressed, with the suppression recorded in the spool so the record stays
# complete. --resolve clears the key and sends one recovery notice, so a
# condition that fixes itself says so rather than going quiet in a way that is
# indistinguishable from the alerter dying.
#
# EXIT CODES
#   0  delivered to the push sink
#   3  no push sink configured. The journal, the spool and the dedupe stamp were
#      still written, so the external watcher WILL see this within one of its
#      intervals. 3 means "not pushed", not "nobody will ever know", and the
#      difference matters now that a second path exists.
#   4  a push sink is configured and delivery FAILED
#   5  suppressed as a duplicate inside the repeat window
#   6  --ack named a key that is not pending. Nothing was acknowledged, and
#      saying so is the whole point: a silent success here would leave an
#      operator believing a condition was acknowledged when it was not.
#
# Never non-zero for a bad argument once the message exists: an alerter that
# refuses to alert because of its own argument handling is the failure mode this
# whole file exists to avoid.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
set -uo pipefail

ALERT_ENV=${PULLFM_ALERT_ENV:-/etc/pullfm/alert.env}
SPOOL_DIR=${PULLFM_ALERT_SPOOL_DIR:-/var/log/pullfm}
STATE_DIR=${PULLFM_ALERT_STATE_DIR:-/var/lib/pullfm/alerts}
SPOOL=${SPOOL_DIR}/alerts.jsonl

KEY=""
TITLE=""
MESSAGE=""
PRIORITY=high
TAGS=rotating_light
RUNBOOK=""
RESOLVE=false
ACK=false
ACK_NOTE=""
LIST=false

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --key) KEY=${2:-}; shift 2 ;;
    --title) TITLE=${2:-}; shift 2 ;;
    --message) MESSAGE=${2:-}; shift 2 ;;
    --priority) PRIORITY=${2:-high}; shift 2 ;;
    --tags) TAGS=${2:-rotating_light}; shift 2 ;;
    --runbook) RUNBOOK=${2:-}; shift 2 ;;
    --resolve) RESOLVE=true; shift ;;
    # --ack takes the key as its own argument so the acknowledging command reads
    # as one thing: `pullfm-alert --ack unit:pullfm-backup`. It also sets KEY, so
    # the same sanitiser produces the same filename it did when the alert fired.
    --ack) ACK=true; KEY=${2:-}; shift 2 ;;
    --ack-note) ACK_NOTE=${2:-}; shift 2 ;;
    --list | --pending) LIST=true; shift ;;
    --) shift; break ;;
    *) shift ;;
  esac
done

[ -n "${TITLE}" ] || TITLE="Pull.fm alert"
[ -n "${KEY}" ] || KEY=$(printf '%s' "${TITLE}" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9' '_')
# Only the characters that are safe in a filename. The key reaches the
# filesystem, so it is sanitised rather than trusted.
SAFE_KEY=$(printf '%s' "${KEY}" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9._-' '_' | cut -c1-96)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --ack and --list
#
# Placed BEFORE the stdin read below on purpose. That read consumes stdin
# whenever it is not a terminal, so an --ack run from a script or a timer would
# otherwise block or swallow unrelated input, and an acknowledgement command that
# hangs is one an operator stops using.
#
# `pullfm-alert` is the only writer of this directory's layout, so the layout is
# documented here rather than inferred anywhere else:
#
#   <key>         the dedupe stamp. ONE Unix timestamp. Never touched by an ack.
#   <key>.first   the epoch this key was FIRST seen. Written once. Gives age.
#   <key>.ack     an acknowledgement record: epoch, who, host, note.
#
# pullfm-heartbeat skips the two sibling suffixes when it counts, and skips any
# key whose .ack exists. Those two rules are what make `pending` mean
# "unacknowledged" rather than "ever fired".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ACK_SUFFIX=.ack
FIRST_SUFFIX=.first

# The one sanitiser, used everywhere a key becomes a filename. pullfm-heartbeat
# carries an identical copy for the same reason: if the two ever disagree, an ack
# is written under one spelling and looked up under another, and an
# acknowledgement that is recorded but never read is worse than no ack verb.
# The '-' is LAST in the set on purpose; anywhere else tr reads it as a range.
safe_key() { printf '%s\n' "$(printf '%s' "${1}" | tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9._-' '_' | cut -c1-96)"; }

# Who is doing the acknowledging. SUDO_USER first, because these commands are
# almost always run through sudo and recording "root" for every ack would make
# the record useless for the one question it exists to answer.
ack_who() {
  printf '%s' "${PULLFM_ACK_WHO:-${SUDO_USER:-${USER:-$(id -un 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)}}}"
}

# Age of a key in seconds, from .first when it exists. Falls back to the dedupe
# stamp and then to the file mtime, so a key created before .first existed still
# reports an age rather than reporting nothing. An unknown age prints as -1 and
# is never silently rendered as 0, because 0 reads as "brand new" and would make
# an old condition look fresh.
key_age_s() {
  _ka_f="${STATE_DIR}/${1}${FIRST_SUFFIX}"
  _ka_s="${STATE_DIR}/${1}"
  _ka_e=""
  if [ -f "${_ka_f}" ]; then
    _ka_e=$(cat "${_ka_f}" 2>/dev/null || true)
  elif [ -f "${_ka_s}" ]; then
    _ka_e=$(cat "${_ka_s}" 2>/dev/null || true)
  fi
  case "${_ka_e}" in '' | *[!0-9]*)
    if [ -e "${_ka_s}" ]; then
      _ka_e=$(stat -c %Y "${_ka_s}" 2>/dev/null || stat -f %m "${_ka_s}" 2>/dev/null || true)
    fi
    ;;
  esac
  case "${_ka_e}" in '' | *[!0-9]*) printf '%s' -1; return 0 ;; esac
  printf '%s' "$(($(date -u +%s) - _ka_e))"
}

# Every pending key, from BOTH sources the heartbeat counts, so --list and the
# beat can never disagree about what is outstanding. A list that showed a
# different set from the thing raising the alarm would send an operator looking
# for a key that is not there.
pending_keys() {
  if [ -d "${STATE_DIR}" ]; then
    for _pf in "${STATE_DIR}"/*; do
      [ -e "${_pf}" ] || continue
      case "${_pf}" in *"${ACK_SUFFIX}" | *"${FIRST_SUFFIX}") continue ;; esac
      printf '%s\n' "$(basename "${_pf}")"
    done
  fi
  systemctl list-units "${PULLFM_HEARTBEAT_UNIT_GLOB:-pullfm-*}" \
    --state=failed --plain --no-legend --no-pager 2>/dev/null |
    while read -r _pu _rest; do
      [ -n "${_pu}" ] || continue
      # tr is applied to ONE key with no newline in it, and the set ends with the
      # literal '-'. Both matter: the first version piped a newline-terminated
      # stream through `tr -c 'a-zA-Z0-9._-\n' '_'`, where `_-\n` is read as a
      # RANGE, so tr refused the whole expression with "range-endpoints ... in
      # reverse collating sequence order" and this function emitted nothing.
      # --list then printed "(nothing pending)" while the beat correctly reported
      # pending=1 - a listing tool disagreeing with the alarm it exists to
      # explain. Found by running it against a real failed unit.
      safe_key "unit:${_pu%.service}"
    done
}

if [ "${LIST}" = true ]; then
  _n=0
  printf '%-46s %-10s %s\n' KEY AGE STATUS
  for _k in $(pending_keys | sort -u); do
    _n=$((_n + 1))
    _age=$(key_age_s "${_k}")
    if [ "${_age}" -lt 0 ]; then _agetxt="unknown"; else _agetxt="$((_age / 60))m"; fi
    if [ -f "${STATE_DIR}/${_k}${ACK_SUFFIX}" ]; then
      _st="ACKED by $(cut -f2 <"${STATE_DIR}/${_k}${ACK_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || echo '?')"
    else
      _st="unacknowledged"
    fi
    printf '%-46s %-10s %s\n' "${_k}" "${_agetxt}" "${_st}"
  done
  [ "${_n}" -eq 0 ] && echo "(nothing pending)"
  echo
  echo "acknowledge with: pullfm-alert --ack <key> [--ack-note 'why']"
  echo "a condition that is FIXED should be cleared with --resolve, or with"
  echo "systemctl reset-failed for a unit: key. ack means seen, not fixed."
  exit 0
fi

if [ "${ACK}" = true ]; then
  if [ -z "${KEY}" ]; then
    echo "pullfm-alert --ack needs a key. See what is pending with: pullfm-alert --list" >&2
    exit 6
  fi
  # Is this key actually pending? Checked against the same two sources the
  # heartbeat counts, so an ack can never appear to succeed against a key that
  # nothing is reporting.
  _found=false
  for _k in $(pending_keys | sort -u); do
    [ "${_k}" = "${SAFE_KEY}" ] && _found=true && break
  done
  if [ "${_found}" != true ]; then
    echo "pullfm-alert: '${KEY}' is not pending, so nothing was acknowledged." >&2
    echo "Pending keys:" >&2
    pending_keys | sort -u | sed 's/^/  /' >&2
    exit 6
  fi

  mkdir -p "${STATE_DIR}" 2>/dev/null || true
  _who=$(ack_who)
  _age=$(key_age_s "${SAFE_KEY}")
  # -1 is the internal sentinel for "no first-seen is recorded", which is the
  # normal case for a systemd-sourced unit: key. It is rendered as the word
  # rather than as "-1s", because a negative duration in an incident record
  # reads as a bug in the recorder and invites someone to distrust the rest.
  if [ "${_age}" -lt 0 ]; then _agetxt=unknown; else _agetxt="${_age}s"; fi
  # Tab separated and one line, so `cut -f2` reads the who without a parser and a
  # note containing a quote cannot corrupt the record. Tabs and newlines are
  # stripped from the note for the same reason.
  _note=$(printf '%s' "${ACK_NOTE}" | tr '\t\n\r' '   ')
  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' \
    "$(date -u +%s)" "${_who}" "$(hostname 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)" \
    "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" "${_note}" \
    >"${STATE_DIR}/${SAFE_KEY}${ACK_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || {
    echo "pullfm-alert: could not write the acknowledgement record. NOT acknowledged." >&2
    exit 6
  }
  chmod 0644 "${STATE_DIR}/${SAFE_KEY}${ACK_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || true

  # The journal gets the ack too. The acknowledgement is part of the incident
  # record, and the spool is written by the alerting paths above; an ack that
  # existed only as a file would be invisible to `journalctl -t pullfm-alert`,
  # which is where every runbook already sends the reader.
  echo "PULLFM-ALERT acknowledged key=${KEY} by=${_who} age=${_agetxt} note=${_note:-none}" >&2
  logger -t pullfm-alert -p user.notice \
    "acknowledged key=${KEY} by=${_who} age=${_agetxt} note=${_note:-none}" 2>/dev/null || true

  echo "acknowledged ${KEY} (by ${_who})."
  echo "The dedupe stamp is untouched, so the repeat window still applies, and this"
  echo "will return to unacknowledged if the condition fires again."
  # Refresh the beat so `pending` drops now rather than at the next timer tick.
  command -v pullfm-heartbeat >/dev/null 2>&1 && pullfm-heartbeat >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  exit 0
fi

# stdin is accepted so a caller can pipe a long body without quoting it.
if [ -z "${MESSAGE}" ] && [ ! -t 0 ]; then
  MESSAGE=$(cat 2>/dev/null || true)
fi
[ -n "${MESSAGE}" ] || MESSAGE="(no detail)"

WHEN=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
NOW=$(date -u +%s)
HOST=$(hostname 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)

if [ -f "${ALERT_ENV}" ]; then
  # shellcheck disable=SC1090
  . "${ALERT_ENV}" 2>/dev/null || true
fi

REPEAT_S=${PULLFM_ALERT_REPEAT_S:-3600}
ENVLABEL=${PULLFM_ALERT_ENV_LABEL:-unknown}
STATE_FILE="${STATE_DIR}/${SAFE_KEY}"

mkdir -p "${STATE_DIR}" 2>/dev/null || true
mkdir -p "${SPOOL_DIR}" 2>/dev/null || true

# --- spool writer, used on every path -------------------------------------
# Values are JSON-escaped through the same helper so a journal tail containing a
# quote cannot produce a spool line that no parser will read. A spool nothing
# can parse is a spool that does not exist.
esc() {
  printf '%s' "${1:-}" | python3 -c 'import json,sys; sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(sys.stdin.read())[1:-1])' 2>/dev/null ||
    printf '%s' "${1:-}" | tr -d '"\\' | tr '\n\r\t' '   '
}

spool() {
  printf '{"ts":"%s","host":"%s","env":"%s","key":"%s","title":"%s","priority":"%s","resolve":%s,"delivered":%s,"delivery":"%s"}\n' \
    "${WHEN}" "$(esc "${HOST}")" "$(esc "${ENVLABEL}")" "$(esc "${KEY}")" \
    "$(esc "${TITLE}")" "${PRIORITY}" "${RESOLVE}" "${1}" "${2}" \
    >>"${SPOOL}" 2>/dev/null || true
  chmod 0640 "${SPOOL}" 2>/dev/null || true
}

# --- resolve path ----------------------------------------------------------
if [ "${RESOLVE}" = true ]; then
  # Nothing was firing, so there is nothing to recover from and no notice is
  # owed. Staying silent here is what keeps the channel worth reading.
  [ -f "${STATE_FILE}" ] || exit 0
  rm -f "${STATE_FILE}" 2>/dev/null || true
  # The siblings go with it. A resolved condition that left its .ack behind would
  # come back already-acknowledged the next time it fired, which is the one state
  # this design must never produce: a firing alert that nothing counts.
  rm -f "${STATE_FILE}${ACK_SUFFIX}" "${STATE_FILE}${FIRST_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || true
  PRIORITY=default
  TAGS=white_check_mark
  TITLE="RESOLVED: ${TITLE}"
else
  # --- dedupe ---------------------------------------------------------------
  if [ -f "${STATE_FILE}" ]; then
    LAST=$(cat "${STATE_FILE}" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)
    case "${LAST}" in ''|*[!0-9]*) LAST=0 ;; esac
    if [ $((NOW - LAST)) -lt "${REPEAT_S}" ]; then
      echo "PULLFM-ALERT suppressed key=${KEY} (last sent $((NOW - LAST))s ago, repeat ${REPEAT_S}s)" >&2
      spool false suppressed-duplicate
      exit 5
    fi
  fi
fi

# --- 1. the journal --------------------------------------------------------
{
  echo "PULLFM-ALERT env=${ENVLABEL} host=${HOST} key=${KEY} priority=${PRIORITY}"
  echo "${TITLE}"
  echo "${MESSAGE}"
  [ -n "${RUNBOOK}" ] && echo "runbook: ${RUNBOOK}"
} >&2

# --- 3. delivery -----------------------------------------------------------
DELIVERED=false
DELIVERY=none-configured

# PULLFM_NTFY_URL is still honoured so a node whose alert.env predates the
# provider-agnostic rename keeps pushing rather than silently stopping. A
# compatibility fallback that is written down is maintenance; one that is not is
# a trap.
SINK_URL=${PULLFM_ALERT_SINK_URL:-${PULLFM_NTFY_URL:-}}
SINK_TOKEN=${PULLFM_ALERT_SINK_TOKEN:-${PULLFM_NTFY_TOKEN:-}}
SINK_KIND=${PULLFM_ALERT_SINK_KIND:-}
[ -n "${SINK_KIND}" ] || SINK_KIND=ntfy

if [ -n "${SINK_URL}" ]; then
  DELIVERY=attempted

  BODY="${MESSAGE}"
  [ -n "${RUNBOOK}" ] && BODY="${BODY}

runbook: ${RUNBOOK}"
  BODY="${BODY}

env=${ENVLABEL} host=${HOST} key=${KEY} at=${WHEN}"

  # An array, not `${VAR:+-H "..."}`. The inline form looks like it works and
  # does not: an unquoted expansion word-splits on the spaces inside the header
  # value, so curl receives four broken arguments instead of two.
  args=()
  case "${SINK_KIND}" in
    ntfy)
      args=(-H "Title: ${TITLE}" -H "Priority: ${PRIORITY}" -H "Tags: ${TAGS}")
      [ -n "${RUNBOOK}" ] && args+=(-H "Actions: view, Runbook, ${RUNBOOK}")
      [ -n "${SINK_TOKEN}" ] && args+=(-H "Authorization: Bearer ${SINK_TOKEN}")
      PAYLOAD="${BODY}"
      ;;
    discord | slack | webhook)
      # One JSON body that all three accept. Discord reads `content`, Slack reads
      # `text`, and a generic receiver gets both plus the structured fields. Three
      # nearly identical payload builders would be three places for the escaping
      # to go wrong, and the escaping is the only hard part.
      esc_title=$(esc "${TITLE}")
      esc_body=$(esc "${BODY}")
      PAYLOAD=$(printf '{"content":"**%s**\\n%s","text":"*%s*\\n%s","username":"pullfm-%s","pullfm":{"key":"%s","priority":"%s","env":"%s","host":"%s","resolved":%s}}' \
        "${esc_title}" "${esc_body}" "${esc_title}" "${esc_body}" \
        "$(esc "${ENVLABEL}")" "$(esc "${KEY}")" "${PRIORITY}" \
        "$(esc "${ENVLABEL}")" "$(esc "${HOST}")" "${RESOLVE}")
      args=(-H 'Content-Type: application/json')
      # A bearer header is added only when a token exists. Discord and Slack
      # authenticate by the secret INSIDE the URL and reject an extra
      # Authorization header on some paths, which is why it is conditional rather
      # than always present.
      [ -n "${SINK_TOKEN}" ] && args+=(-H "Authorization: Bearer ${SINK_TOKEN}")
      ;;
    *)
      DELIVERY=unknown-sink-kind
      echo "PULLFM-ALERT sink kind '${SINK_KIND}' is not supported by this version." >&2
      SINK_URL=""
      ;;
  esac

  # --max-time 10 twice over: an alerter that blocks is a watchdog that stops
  # watching. Two attempts, because a single dropped SYN should not be the
  # difference between told and not told, and no more than two because the spool
  # and the heartbeat are already the durable record.
  if [ -n "${SINK_URL}" ]; then
    for attempt in 1 2; do
      if printf '%s' "${PAYLOAD}" | curl -fsS --max-time 10 \
        "${args[@]+"${args[@]}"}" \
        --data-binary @- \
        "${SINK_URL}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        DELIVERED=true
        DELIVERY="${SINK_KIND}"
        break
      fi
      [ "${attempt}" = 1 ] && sleep 2
    done

    [ "${DELIVERED}" = true ] || {
      DELIVERY="${SINK_KIND}-failed"
      echo "PULLFM-ALERT push to the ${SINK_KIND} sink FAILED. The journal, ${SPOOL} and the" >&2
      echo "heartbeat are the record, and the external watcher will still see this alert." >&2
    }
  fi
fi

# --- 2. the spool ----------------------------------------------------------
# Written after the attempt so `delivered` is a fact rather than an intention.
spool "${DELIVERED}" "${DELIVERY}"

# The dedupe stamp is only set for a firing alert, and only once the attempt is
# over. Setting it earlier would let a delivery failure consume the whole repeat
# window, so a transient network fault would silence a real condition for an
# hour.
if [ "${RESOLVE}" != true ]; then
  printf '%s' "${NOW}" >"${STATE_FILE}" 2>/dev/null || true

  # First-seen, written ONCE and never rewritten. This is what gives an
  # unacknowledged condition an age, and an age is what lets a stale one
  # escalate instead of sitting at the same count forever.
  [ -f "${STATE_FILE}${FIRST_SUFFIX}" ] ||
    printf '%s' "${NOW}" >"${STATE_FILE}${FIRST_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || true
  chmod 0644 "${STATE_FILE}${FIRST_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || true

  # A REAL re-fire clears a previous acknowledgement, so the condition returns to
  # unacknowledged and the switch counts it again.
  #
  # Reaching this line already means the alert was NOT suppressed as a duplicate:
  # the dedupe branch above exits 5 long before here. So the repeat window has
  # expired and this is news again, not the same page repeating. That distinction
  # is the whole reason the ack lives in a sibling file rather than in the dedupe
  # stamp - an ack silences the nagging without silencing the condition.
  if [ -f "${STATE_FILE}${ACK_SUFFIX}" ]; then
    _prev_ack=$(cut -f2 <"${STATE_FILE}${ACK_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)
    rm -f "${STATE_FILE}${ACK_SUFFIX}" 2>/dev/null || true
    echo "PULLFM-ALERT key=${KEY} fired again after being acknowledged by ${_prev_ack}; ack cleared." >&2
  fi
fi

# --- refresh the heartbeat so the external watcher sees this within its next
#     interval rather than within the heartbeat timer's -----------------------
#
# The stamp above is what pullfm-heartbeat counts, so the alert would reach the
# watcher anyway at the next five-minute beat. Refreshing here removes up to five
# minutes of avoidable latency on the path that always works, and it is the
# cheapest thing in this file.
#
# Guarded and ignored on failure ON PURPOSE. An alerter that fails because its
# heartbeat emitter is missing is an alerter that stops alerting over a
# non-essential optimisation, and the timer will emit the beat regardless.
if command -v pullfm-heartbeat >/dev/null 2>&1; then
  pullfm-heartbeat >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
fi

[ "${DELIVERED}" = true ] && exit 0
[ "${DELIVERY}" = none-configured ] && exit 3
exit 4
